From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Er Gibbio <gibbio@pm.me>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 22:03:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] display: cap the reported margin while video follows
 the audio, not for MJPEG

Following the audio clock, a stream's margin is about the audio's delay,
which changes with the output device (~280 ms with AirPods, ~130 with the
built-in output) and peaks for a moment when it does. spice-server's
GStreamer rate control took the 408 ms of a switch from AirPods to the
built-in output as the stream's best margin and lowered the bit rate 115
times in a minute against the 131 ms after it, dropping frames itself.
Report at most the default 50 ms there too.

MJPEG streams go back to the margins as they were before the cap (0011):
spice-server's MJPEG rate control is different, asks the client for a
longer delay whenever the margin is below about two frames, and was fine
with them.
---
 src/channel-display.c | 35 +++++++++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/channel-display.c b/src/channel-display.c
index 7aeb4bc..0ebdb70 100644
--- a/src/channel-display.c
+++ b/src/channel-display.c
@@ -2195,18 +2195,29 @@ static void display_handle_stream_data(SpiceChannel *channel, SpiceMsgIn *in)
      * clock they set up, newer ones the delay the frame really gets, rather than a
      * client that seems unable to keep up: their rate control cuts the stream
      * whenever the reported delay is below -15 ms (to ~1 Mbit/s in our test). */
-    /* On its own clock, a frame is shown `target` ms after arrival, whatever
-     * its slack, so tell the server the margin the default target would give,
-     * and no more: a viewer's choice of target (Video Delay) and a frame that
-     * came early are no news for its rate control, which judges each report
-     * against the largest margin the stream has had and lowers the bit rate,
-     * report after report, while margins stay below half of it. Uncapped,
-     * ~150 ms during a stream's first second, then 50, took H.264 streams to
-     * ~0.1 Mbit/s and 3 fps within a minute; after a change from 80 to 30 ms
-     * it halved the bit rate. A frame arriving late still lowers the margin. */
-    server_margin = (c->playout_target > 0 && !c->playout_audio) ?
-        MAX(margin_report, MIN(margin - c->playout_target, 0) + PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS) :
-        MAX(margin_report, margin);
+    /* spice-server's GStreamer rate control (H.264...) judges each report
+     * against the largest margin the stream has had, and lowers the bit rate,
+     * report after report, while margins stay below half of it. So tell it the
+     * margin the default target would give, and no more:
+     * - on its own clock, a frame is shown `target` ms after arrival, whatever
+     *   its slack: a viewer's choice of target (Video Delay) and a frame that
+     *   came early are no news. Uncapped, ~150 ms during a stream's first
+     *   second, then 50, took H.264 to ~0.1 Mbit/s and 3 fps within a minute;
+     *   a change from 80 to 30 ms halved the bit rate;
+     * - following the audio, the margin is about the audio's delay, which
+     *   changes with the output device (~280 ms with AirPods, ~130 built in)
+     *   and peaks for a moment when it does: from 408 to 131 ms, the server
+     *   lowered the bit rate 115 times in a minute.
+     * A frame arriving late still lowers the margin. MJPEG streams keep the
+     * margins as they are: the server's MJPEG rate control works differently,
+     * and asks for a longer delay when the margin is below two frames. */
+    if (st->video_decoder->codec_type == SPICE_VIDEO_CODEC_TYPE_MJPEG) {
+        server_margin = MAX(margin_report, margin);
+    } else if (c->playout_target > 0 && !c->playout_audio) {
+        server_margin = MAX(margin_report, MIN(margin - c->playout_target, 0) + PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS);
+    } else {
+        server_margin = MIN(MAX(margin_report, margin), PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS);
+    }
     margin_report = MAX(margin_report, margin);
     if (margin > 0) {
         SpiceSession *s = spice_channel_get_session(channel);
